\maketitle

<<setup, include=FALSE, cache=FALSE>>=
library(DRwinStats)
knitr::opts_chunk$set(cache = FALSE, collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>",
                     fig.width = 7, fig.height = 4.5,
                     fig.align = "center", out.width = "0.95\\linewidth")
@

\section{Data and clinical comparison}

\texttt{DRwinStats} estimates win, loss, and tie probabilities for a prespecified
hierarchy of survival outcomes. The win ratio, win odds, and net benefit are
calculated from these probabilities. Participants are compared through a
common restriction time, and longer event-free times are preferred. The same
interface is used for individually randomized and cluster-randomized trials.
When no cluster variable is supplied, each participant is treated as a
separate cluster.

The input is a data frame containing longitudinal event records. Each row
gives a participant identifier, treatment assignment, time, and event code.
Event code \texttt{1} is the terminal event of highest priority, usually death.
Codes \texttt{2}, \texttt{3}, and subsequent codes identify nonterminal events
in decreasing order of priority. Code \texttt{0} records the end of observation.
The code does not identify the treatment arm.

Each participant must have one final row with code \texttt{0} or \texttt{1}.
Earlier rows record nonterminal events. For example, hospitalization followed
by death gives a code-2 row followed by a code-1 row. Hospitalization followed
by the end of observation gives a code-2 row followed by a code-0 row.
A hospitalization row alone does not establish the duration of subsequent
follow-up. Baseline covariates and treatment assignment are repeated across
the participant's rows. In a CRT, treatment assignment must be constant
within each cluster.

The example datasets contain simulated records only. The IRT has 500
participants, with 250 in each arm. The CRT has 334 participants in 30
clusters, with 15 clusters in each arm. Both examples prioritize death over
first hospitalization and have administrative follow-up at three years.

<<data>>=
utils::data("example_irt", package = "DRwinStats",
            envir = environment())
utils::data("example_crt", package = "DRwinStats",
            envir = environment())
head(example_irt)
length(unique(example_irt$id))
length(unique(example_crt$cluster))
@

With \texttt{recurrent = "first"}, the first occurrence of each nonterminal
event type defines its event time. The package does not interpret repeated
rows as a count-based recurrent-event comparison. The \texttt{priorities}
argument specifies the event codes to include; supplying it explicitly also
identifies an outcome with no observed event in a particular dataset.
The default zero thresholds distinguish any unequal restricted times.
Positive thresholds can be supplied when the clinical comparison requires
a minimum time difference. See \texttt{help("drwins")} for the supported
model and outcome combinations.

\subsection{End of observation and restriction times}

A status-0 row at time $u$ indicates that the participant was observed through
$u$. At a restriction time $\tau\leq u$, the restricted outcome is therefore
observed. At $\tau>u$, that row indicates censoring before the restricted
outcome is resolved. A terminal event before $\tau$ resolves the restricted
outcome. This distinction is used both in estimation and in the reported
censoring rates.

If \texttt{times} is omitted, the package uses the 25th, 50th, and 75th
percentiles of the participants' final follow-up times. Each participant
contributes once to this calculation, even if several event rows are present.
Repeated quantiles are removed. These defaults provide convenient example
analyses; the restriction times for a trial analysis should reflect its
scientific objectives.

<<followup>>=
irt_end <- example_irt[example_irt$status %in% c(0L, 1L), ]
quantile(irt_end$time, probs = c(0.25, 0.50, 0.75))

# Proportion censored before the restricted outcome is resolved at two years.
mean(irt_end$status == 0L & irt_end$time < 2)
@

\section{Individually randomized trials}

The \texttt{outcome} and \texttt{censoring} arguments accept one-sided model
formulas. A common outcome formula is applied to each component. Treatment
need not be included in either formula because the models are fitted
separately in the two treatment arms. Formula terms can include factors,
interactions, and transformations such as \texttt{I(x\^{}2)}.

The following analysis uses proportional hazards outcome margins, a Gumbel
survival copula, and a Cox censoring model. The examples were generated with
fixed equal allocation, so \texttt{allocation = "complete"} is specified.

<<irt-analysis, eval=FALSE>>=
fit_irt <- DRwinStats::drwins(
  data = example_irt,
  outcome = ~ age + sex + x,
  censoring = ~ age + sex + x,
  id = "id", time = "time", status = "status",
  treatment = "treatment",
  priorities = c(1L, 2L),
  outcome_model = "cox",
  censoring_model = "cox",
  copula = "gumbel",
  times = c(1, 2, 3),
  allocation = "complete"
)
summary(fit_irt)
plot(fit_irt, measure = "WR")
@

Column names can be changed through \texttt{id}, \texttt{time},
\texttt{status}, \texttt{treatment}, and \texttt{cluster}. For a treatment
variable with labels other than 0 and 1, \texttt{treatment\_levels} specifies
the control and treatment levels in that order. For independently randomized
units, use \texttt{allocation = "independent"} and the known assignment
probability in \texttt{treatment\_probability}. The default probability is
$1/2$.

\subsection{A small reproducible fit}

The following code illustrates the fitted object with 40 participants from
the example data. It uses Kaplan--Meier models and independence between the
component event times. Both model formulas are intercept-only. This is a
quick illustration of the interface rather than the covariate-adjusted
analysis above.

<<small-data>>=
first_ids <- unlist(lapply(split(irt_end$id, irt_end$treatment),
                          function(z) head(z, 20L)), use.names = FALSE)
small_irt <- example_irt[example_irt$id %in% first_ids, ]
@

<<small-fit>>=
fit_small <- DRwinStats::drwins(
  small_irt,
  outcome = ~ 1, censoring = ~ 1,
  outcome_model = "km", censoring_model = "km",
  copula = "independence", priorities = c(1L, 2L),
  times = c(1, 1.5, 2), allocation = "complete"
)
summary(fit_small)
@

<<small-plot>>=
plot(fit_small, measure = "NB")
@

\section{Cluster-randomized trials}

Supplying \texttt{cluster} identifies the independent randomization units.
The cluster-average estimand gives each cluster equal weight. The
individual-average estimand gives each participant equal weight. When
\texttt{target = "both"}, the fitted object contains both estimands.
They can differ when cluster size is associated with prognosis or treatment
response.

An outcome formula can include participant and cluster covariates. Cluster
size is not added automatically as a covariate; it can be included explicitly
when scientifically appropriate. The target-population weights are handled
separately from these regression choices.

<<crt-analysis, eval=FALSE>>=
fit_crt <- DRwinStats::drwins(
  data = example_crt,
  outcome = ~ age + sex + x + cluster_cov + log(cluster_size),
  censoring = ~ age + sex + x + cluster_cov,
  cluster = "cluster",
  priorities = c(1L, 2L),
  outcome_model = "cox", censoring_model = "cox",
  copula = "gumbel",
  times = c(1, 2, 3), target = "both",
  allocation = "complete"
)
summary(fit_crt, measure = "WR")
plot(fit_crt, measure = "WR")
@

The variance calculation treats each cluster as one independent unit.
Participants from the same cluster are kept together. Consequently, the
number of clusters determines the large-sample approximation, even though
many participant comparisons contribute to the estimator. If a dataset is
reduced for a trial analysis or a computational example, retain whole
clusters rather than selecting individual records within each cluster.

\section{Choosing the working models}

\subsection{Formulas for different outcomes}

A named list permits a different formula for each priority. The list names
are the event codes.

<<outcome-formulas, eval=FALSE>>=
outcome_formulas <- list(
  `1` = ~ age + sex + x,
  `2` = ~ age + sex + x + I(x^2)
)

fit_separate <- DRwinStats::drwins(
  example_irt,
  outcome = outcome_formulas,
  censoring = ~ age + sex + x,
  priorities = c(1L, 2L),
  copula = "gumbel", times = c(1, 2, 3),
  allocation = "complete"
)
@

\subsection{Cox and Kaplan--Meier models}

The Cox option accommodates the covariates in the supplied formula. The
Kaplan--Meier option is a product-limit estimator and requires an
intercept-only formula. A Kaplan--Meier censoring model can be combined with
an outcome model that includes covariates. Kaplan--Meier outcome margins are
used with the independence copula. For a joint copula model without outcome
covariates, use \texttt{outcome\_model = "nonparametric"} and
\texttt{outcome = \textasciitilde{} 1}; the margins are then estimated through
the joint outcome likelihood.

<<km-and-nonparametric, eval=FALSE>>=
fit_km_censoring <- DRwinStats::drwins(
  example_irt,
  outcome = ~ age + sex + x, censoring = ~ 1,
  censoring_model = "km", copula = "gumbel",
  priorities = c(1L, 2L), times = c(1, 2, 3),
  allocation = "complete"
)

fit_no_outcome_covariates <- DRwinStats::drwins(
  example_irt,
  outcome = ~ 1, censoring = ~ age + sex + x,
  outcome_model = "nonparametric", copula = "clayton",
  priorities = c(1L, 2L), times = c(1, 2, 3),
  allocation = "complete"
)
@

\subsection{Dependence between event times}

The available copula choices are \texttt{gumbel}, \texttt{clayton},
\texttt{frank}, \texttt{joe}, and \texttt{independence}. The fitted copulas
describe dependence among outcomes of the same participant. Dependence
between participants in a CRT is accounted for in the cluster-level
variance calculation. The implemented Archimedean families use their
positive-dependence parameter ranges; independence is available explicitly.
The chosen copula and its fitted parameters are retained in the fitted
object.

Changing the copula specifies a different joint outcome model and can be
used for a sensitivity analysis. The censoring model and clinical comparison
can be held fixed across such analyses.

<<copula-sensitivity, eval=FALSE>>=
fit_frank <- DRwinStats::drwins(
  example_irt,
  outcome = ~ age + sex + x, censoring = ~ age + sex + x,
  copula = "frank", priorities = c(1L, 2L),
  times = c(1, 2, 3), allocation = "complete"
)
summary(fit_frank, measure = "WR")
@

\subsection{Optional ridge penalties}

Ridge regularization is controlled explicitly. A zero value turns it off.
A positive value can be useful when the covariates lead to unstable model
fits. The selected value is stored with the fit so the analysis can be
reproduced.

<<ridge-option, eval=FALSE>>=
fit_ridge <- DRwinStats::drwins(
  example_irt,
  outcome = ~ age + sex + x, censoring = ~ age + sex + x,
  copula = "gumbel", priorities = c(1L, 2L),
  times = c(1, 2, 3), allocation = "complete",
  control = DRwinStats::drwins_control(ridge = 0.01)
)
@

The default control uses \texttt{ridge = 0}. Inspect the fit diagnostics when
changing the working models or the penalty. Regularization addresses
numerical stability; the double robustness result concerns correct
specification of the censoring models or the joint outcome models.

\section{Results, censoring rates, and plots}

\texttt{summary()} displays the estimated probabilities and win summaries,
standard errors, confidence intervals, and censoring information.
\texttt{as.data.frame()} provides the estimates in a data frame.
\texttt{coef()}, \texttt{confint()}, and \texttt{vcov()} provide standard
access to coefficients, confidence intervals, and the covariance of the
estimated win and loss probabilities.

<<accessors, eval=FALSE>>=
summary(fit_irt, measure = "WR")
as.data.frame(fit_irt)
coef(fit_irt, measure = "NB")
confint(fit_irt, measure = "WR")
vcov(fit_irt)
fit_irt$censoring
@

The censoring table is indexed by restriction time and treatment arm and
identifies the population weights used. Rates refer to participants censored
before their restricted outcome is resolved. The proportion of all rows with
status 0 is not a censoring rate because a participant may contribute several
rows and administrative follow-up at or beyond the restriction time resolves
the outcome.

Plots can show the win ratio, win odds, net benefit, or comparison
probabilities. The default colors are muted and distinguish the two CRT
targets. Intervals for the win ratio and win odds are calculated on the
logarithmic scale and then transformed back. Intervals at different
restriction times are pointwise.

<<plots, eval=FALSE>>=
plot(fit_irt, measure = "WR")
plot(fit_irt, measure = "WO")
plot(fit_irt, measure = "NB")
plot(fit_irt, measure = "probabilities")
plot(fit_crt, measure = "WR")
@

For inference, the package retains the contribution of fitting the
censoring and outcome models in the analytic variance calculation. This
contribution is relevant when one set of working models is correctly
specified. For the individual-average CRT probabilities, the calculation
also includes estimation of mean cluster size. The resulting confidence
intervals rely on the stated model and regularity conditions and the number
of independent randomization units.

A win ratio above one favors the treatment arm under the specified hierarchy.
The net benefit is the difference between treatment-win and treatment-loss
probabilities, and the win odds assigns half of the tie probability to each
arm. These are summaries of the same pairwise comparison. Their numerical
values should be interpreted together with the restriction time, clinical
hierarchy, tie probability, and target population.

\section{Further information}

The package help pages document the arguments, fitted-object components,
supported model combinations, and validation rules. The source project
includes the script that generated both example datasets. The statistical
construction follows the accompanying manuscript by Fang and Li,
\emph{Doubly robust win statistics for hierarchical composite endpoints in
individual- and cluster-randomized trials}, and its supplied simulation code.
No clinical treatment effect is implied by the synthetic examples.

For R formula conventions and package structure, see the
\href{https://cran.r-project.org/manuals.html}{R manuals}. The Cox model interface follows
the usual proportional hazards notation used in the \texttt{survival}
package.

\end{document}
